questions.text:
As shown in the figure, an insulated container is fitted with a thermally conducting but immovable partition $\left(P_{1}\right)$ and a freely movable but thermally insulated piston $\left(P_{2}\right)$. The partition $P_{1}$ with thermal conductivity $K$, cross sectional area $A$ and width $x$ divides the container into two sections, $S_{1}$ and $S_{2}$, each containing one mole of a monoatomic gas. The piston $P_{2}$ moves freely such that the gas in $S_{2}$ is always at the atmospheric pressure. Initially, the difference between the temperatures of $S_{1}$ and $S_{2}$ is $\Delta T_{0}$. The time it takes for the temperature difference to become $\frac{\Delta T_{0}}{2}$ is $n x R / K A$, where $R$ is the universal gas constant. The value of $n$ is:
[ Given: $\ln 2 \approx 0.7$ ]
